      "seed": 1905597506,
      "version": 806,
      "versionNonce": 1645748962,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "Htvb9rsjPBVK7f3Wi1rxB",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1785878737677,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Inserting Special Tokens\nStructural signals for where text begins, ends, or switches turns:\n* BOS: Llama 3 uses <|begin_of_text|> = ID 128000.\n* Turn markers: <|start_header_id|> role <|end_header_id|> ... <|eot_id|>.\n* EOS: <|end_of_text|> = 128001 (base), <|eot_id|> = 128009 (chat turn).\n* <s> / </s> are Llama 2 SentencePiece tokens - Llama 3 has none of them.\n\n2. Truncation (Enforcing Limits)\nLlama 3.0 max context = 8,192 tokens; Llama 3.1 = 131,072.\n* Count the prompt tokens, drop the oldest if over the limit.\n* The budget is prompt + generated tokens, not the prompt alone.\n\n3. Padding - a TRAINING-era step, NOT what an inference server does\nPadding into a square matrix belongs to batched training, and to naive\nHF generate() with a left-padded batch. Production engines (vLLM, TGI,\nSGLang) use continuous / ragged batching instead:\n* Sequences are concatenated into one flat token stream plus a cumulative\n  length index (cu_seqlens). No <pad> token is ever inserted.\n* KV memory is allocated in fixed blocks (PagedAttention), not a rectangle.\n* Requests join and leave the running batch every step, so there is no\n  common length to pad to in the first place.\n\n4. Attention Masks\n* With ragged batching there is no padding mask to build. The only mask\n  that always applies is the CAUSAL mask inside the attention block.\n* A padding mask (1 = real token, 0 = pad) exists only in the padded path,\n  and is folded into the same -inf-before-Softmax step as the causal mask.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Inserting Special Tokens\nStructural signals for where text begins, ends, or switches turns:\n* BOS: Llama 3 uses <|begin_of_text|> = ID 128000.\n* Turn markers: <|start_header_id|> role <|end_header_id|> ... <|eot_id|>.\n* EOS: <|end_of_text|> = 128001 (base), <|eot_id|> = 128009 (chat turn).\n* <s> / </s> are Llama 2 SentencePiece tokens - Llama 3 has none of them.\n\n2. Truncation (Enforcing Limits)\nLlama 3.0 max context = 8,192 tokens; Llama 3.1 = 131,072.\n* Count the prompt tokens, drop the oldest if over the limit.\n* The budget is prompt + generated tokens, not the prompt alone.\n\n3. Padding - a TRAINING-era step, NOT what an inference server does\nPadding into a square matrix belongs to batched training, and to naive\nHF generate() with a left-padded batch. Production engines (vLLM, TGI,\nSGLang) use continuous / ragged batching instead:\n* Sequences are concatenated into one flat token stream plus a cumulative\n  length index (cu_seqlens). No <pad> token is ever inserted.\n* KV memory is allocated in fixed blocks (PagedAttention), not a rectangle.\n* Requests join and leave the running batch every step, so there is no\n  common length to pad to in the first place.\n\n4. Attention Masks\n* With ragged batching there is no padding mask to build. The only mask\n  that always applies is the CAUSAL mask inside the attention block.\n* A padding mask (1 = real token, 0 = pad) exists only in the padded path,\n  and is folded into the same -inf-before-Softmax step as the causal mask.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
