        }
      ],
      "updated": 1785878070876,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "OUT:\n    [0.12, -0.45, 0.89 ...............hidden size of model]\n    [0.12, -0.45, 0.89 ...............hidden size of model]\n    .\n    .\n    .\nLast token from the prompt example 1000 token size and 4096 h",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "OUT:\n    [0.12, -0.45, 0.89 ...............hidden size of model]\n    [0.12, -0.45, 0.89 ...............hidden size of model]\n    .\n    .\n    .\nLast token from the prompt example 1000 token size and 4096 h",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HiDhJMf4S7ZlS0eeTz-PD",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2254.3572261945847,
      "y": 1178.5159565881559,
      "width": 880,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2m",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1538000673,
      "version": 439,
      "versionNonce": 608813453,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "J70ZOxQ6DysPYWmJ7H5e9",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1785878737677,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. The Gatekeeper: RMSNorm (input_layernorm)\nLlama 3 adds NO positional vector here. There is no learned or sinusoidal\nposition embedding at the input - the embedding output is position-free.\nPosition enters later, inside EVERY attention block, as RoPE applied to Q and K.\n(Learned absolute position embeddings are GPT-2, a different architecture.)\n\nSo the first thing the embedding output meets is RMSNorm - the first of TWO\nRMSNorm layers in each transformer block (the second sits before the FFN).\n\nWhat it does: scales the numbers inside each token vector to a consistent\nvariance. No mean subtraction and no bias - that is what separates it from\nLayerNorm, and why it is cheaper.\n\nWhy it's there: as data travels through 32 layers the raw numbers can drift\nor grow too large, causing the model to output gibberish. RMSNorm keeps the\nvalues stable and bounded.\n\nHardware: a fast, element-wise, memory-bound kernel on the GPU.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. The Gatekeeper: RMSNorm (input_layernorm)\nLlama 3 adds NO positional vector here. There is no learned or sinusoidal\nposition embedding at the input - the embedding output is position-free.\nPosition enters later, inside EVERY attention block, as RoPE applied to Q and K.\n(Learned absolute position embeddings are GPT-2, a different architecture.)\n\nSo the first thing the embedding output meets is RMSNorm - the first of TWO\nRMSNorm layers in each transformer block (the second sits before the FFN).\n\nWhat it does: scales the numbers inside each token vector to a consistent\nvariance. No mean subtraction and no bias - that is what separates it from\nLayerNorm, and why it is cheaper.\n\nWhy it's there: as data travels through 32 layers the raw numbers can drift\nor grow too large, causing the model to output gibberish. RMSNorm keeps the\nvalues stable and bounded.\n\nHardware: a fast, element-wise, memory-bound kernel on the GPU.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "U-BbYtlk_gPxe_NFDh2tS",
      "type": "text",
