    {
      "id": "ANvh8xQTPXJv_nHpG9Li3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1695.1945284507617,
      "y": 10149.567239907741,
      "width": 402.13121302655946,
      "height": 80.42624260531183,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "text": "RESIDUAL CONNECTION (Skip Path)\nBypasses the entire attention block\nto preserve original identity tokens",
      "fontSize": 22.117216716460785,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "version": 1462,
      "versionNonce": 143538859,
      "index": "b5S",
      "isDeleted": false,
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "seed": 453300446,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "3nfMy5oZxzsYlzjTrGBr7",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1785875545196,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "RESIDUAL CONNECTION (Skip Path)\nBypasses the entire attention block\nto preserve original identity tokens",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.2121212121212122
    },
    {
      "id": "JRztSzEjbI8oVxs9tXr1f",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2963.226909811039,
      "y": 6535.7347933246165,
      "width": 1270.15869140625,
      "height": 550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1164758658,
      "version": 1609,
      "versionNonce": 2079204869,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "UsbCqB5aAj5XkHRL6XKXt",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1785873997421,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. The Logical View: 32 Independent Matrix Sets\n    Conceptually, you can imagine that the model parameters contain 32 \ndistinct small weight tables for every head:\n- Head 0: Has its own W_q0, W_k0, W_v0 → Learns to track subject-verb agreement.\n- Head 1: Has its own W_q1, W_k1, W_v1 → Learns to track punctuation boundaries.\n- Head 31: Has its own W_q31, W_k31, W_v31 → Learns to track temporal context (past vs. future tense).\n\nBecause their weights are completely different, each head projects the exact same incoming token into a \ntotally unique mathematical space.\n\n2. The Physical View: One Giant Matrix (GPU Optimization)\n    While the heads are logically independent, storing 32 separate small matrices in VRAM would slow the GPU \ndown because it would have to launch 32 separate micro-kernels.\n    \n    To maximize memory bandwidth, the GPU fuses all 32 heads into one giant master weight matrix for each \nprojection:\n- Instead of thirty-two 128 × 128 matrices, the GPU stores a single giant 4096 × 4096 matrix.\n- The Execution: The GPU performs one single massive matrix multiplication across this giant table.\n- The Slice: Immediately after that multiplication, the GPU runs a fast memory operation called Reshaping/Transposing. \n\nIt chops the resulting large output vector into 32 equal slices along the column axis and distributes them to the 32 parallel Core\nSelf-Attention Engines.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. The Logical View: 32 Independent Matrix Sets\n    Conceptually, you can imagine that the model parameters contain 32 \ndistinct small weight tables for every head:\n- Head 0: Has its own W_q0, W_k0, W_v0 → Learns to track subject-verb agreement.\n- Head 1: Has its own W_q1, W_k1, W_v1 → Learns to track punctuation boundaries.\n- Head 31: Has its own W_q31, W_k31, W_v31 → Learns to track temporal context (past vs. future tense).\n\nBecause their weights are completely different, each head projects the exact same incoming token into a \ntotally unique mathematical space.\n\n2. The Physical View: One Giant Matrix (GPU Optimization)\n    While the heads are logically independent, storing 32 separate small matrices in VRAM would slow the GPU \ndown because it would have to launch 32 separate micro-kernels.\n    \n    To maximize memory bandwidth, the GPU fuses all 32 heads into one giant master weight matrix for each \nprojection:\n- Instead of thirty-two 128 × 128 matrices, the GPU stores a single giant 4096 × 4096 matrix.\n- The Execution: The GPU performs one single massive matrix multiplication across this giant table.\n- The Slice: Immediately after that multiplication, the GPU runs a fast memory operation called Reshaping/Transposing. \n\nIt chops the resulting large output vector into 32 equal slices along the column axis and distributes them to the 32 parallel Core\nSelf-Attention Engines.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "zTjZ36C-vI_c5OmxzFqJL",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3546.2071501204173,
      "y": 7477.000629316327,
      "width": 772.1134205914075,
      "height": 800,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5x",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 658295390,
      "version": 1460,
      "versionNonce": 1618116263,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "nNr8fkiS6yVNcbWDjCg9M",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1785878111022,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When the 32 individual attention heads finish, they each output a matrix of\nshape [1000 x 128]. The GPU glues (concatenates) these 32 heads side-by-\nside along the columns axis.\n\nSince 128 x 32 = 4096, it forms a single massive [1000 x 4096] matrix.\n\nThe Matrix Multiplication Math\n    The GPU takes this combined attention output and multiplies it by the\nW_o weight matrix, which has a shape of [4096 x 4096].\n\nThe linear algebra calculation looks like this:\n[Sequence Length x Combined Head Dim] * [Combined Head Dim x Hidden Dim]\n_--> [Sequence Length x Hidden Dim]\\\n\n[1000 x 4096] * [4096 x 4096] --> [1000x4096]\n\nThe Final Output Matrix\nThe resulting matrix generated by this layer is exactly [1000 x 4096].\nEvery single one of your original 1,000 prompt tokens has now been returned\nto a vector containing exactly 4,096 numbers.\n\nWhy this specific shape matters\n    This output matrix is structurally identical to the one that entered the\nattention block at the very beginning of the layer. Returning it to this\nstandard [Sequence Length x Hidden Dimension] footprint achieves two\ncritical architectural tasks:\n- It enables the Residual Addition: The GPU can now take the original [1000\nx 4096] input matrix (the skip connection path) and add it element-by-element\nto this new [1000 x 4096] output matrix. You can only add matrices together\nif their shapes match exactly.\n- It fits the Feed-Forward Network (FFN): The next physical layer expects\nthe hidden dimension size to be exactly 4,096 to begin its calculations.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When the 32 individual attention heads finish, they each output a matrix of shape [1000 x 128]. The GPU glues (concatenates) these 32 heads side-by-side along the columns axis.\n\nSince 128 x 32 = 4096, it forms a single massive [1000 x 4096] matrix.\n\nThe Matrix Multiplication Math\n    The GPU takes this combined attention output and multiplies it by the W_o weight matrix, which has a shape of [4096 x 4096].\n\nThe linear algebra calculation looks like this:\n[Sequence Length x Combined Head Dim] * [Combined Head Dim x Hidden Dim] _--> [Sequence Length x Hidden Dim]\\\n\n[1000 x 4096] * [4096 x 4096] --> [1000x4096]\n\nThe Final Output Matrix\nThe resulting matrix generated by this layer is exactly [1000 x 4096].\nEvery single one of your original 1,000 prompt tokens has now been returned to a vector containing exactly 4,096 numbers.\n\nWhy this specific shape matters\n    This output matrix is structurally identical to the one that entered the attention block at the very beginning of the layer. Returning it to this standard [Sequence Length x Hidden Dimension] footprint achieves two critical architectural tasks:\n- It enables the Residual Addition: The GPU can now take the original [1000 x 4096] input matrix (the skip connection path) and add it element-by-element to this new [1000 x 4096] output matrix. You can only add matrices together if their shapes match exactly.\n- It fits the Feed-Forward Network (FFN): The next physical layer expects the hidden dimension size to be exactly 4,096 to begin its calculations.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
