      "updated": 1785878120745,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. It Solves the \"Information Destruction\" Problem\n    Every time data passes through a heavy mathematical layer like Self-Attention, the\noriginal meaning of the text is warped, filtered, and altered.\n\n- The Attention Block is designed to extract context (e.g., how words relate to each\nother).\n- However, in doing so, it can easily corrupt or wash away the core identity of the original\nword.\n\n>> CORRECTION: what the skip path actually carries <<\n    The residual carries x, the RAW pre-norm residual stream - NOT the RMSNorm output.\nThe formula is:   x = x + Attn(RMSNorm(x))\nRMSNorm produces a temporary normalized copy that feeds ONLY the attention block.\nThe untouched x bypasses everything and is what gets added back. The residual stream\nis deliberately never normalized in place - it is the model's persistent memory highway.\n    Mathematically you are saying:\n    \"Give me the deep contextual clues we just discovered, but mix them with a pure copy\nof what the words originally were so we don't forget our starting point.\"\n\n2. It Creates a \"Mathematical Highway\" for Gradients\n    During training (the step where the model learns), error signals called gradients must\ntravel backward through dozens of layers to update the weights.\n\n- The Problem: Passing a gradient backward through the chaotic math of Multi-Head\nAttention, Softmax, and W_o projections acts like a blender. By the time the signal\nreaches the early layers, it gets crushed down to zero (the Vanishing Gradient Problem).\n\n- The Solution: Because adding two matrices together has a calculus derivative of\nexactly 1, the plus sign (+) acts as a frictionless mathematical highway. The learning\nsignals can bypass the messy attention blocks entirely, flowing straight down the skip\nconnections all the way to the front of the model. This allows incredibly deep models (like\n70B or 405B parameter models) to train without their signals degrading.\n(Note: this benefit is TRAINING-only. At inference the residual is purely about\npreserving token identity across 32 layers.)\n\n3. What the Addition Looks Like Numerically\n    Because both matrices have the exact same shape ([1000 x 4096]), the GPU\nperforms an incredibly fast element-wise addition.\n\n    Imagine looking at a single value inside a token vector:\n\n- Original residual-stream value, PRE-norm: 1.5000 (the token's running identity)\n- Out-Projection Attention Value: -0.2300 (The subtle contextual tweak discovered by\nthe 32 heads)\n- Combined Final Value: 1.5000 + (-0.2300) = 1.2700\n\nThe original token's core meaning is preserved, but it has been subtly steered and\nenriched by the attention mechanism.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. It Solves the \"Information Destruction\" Problem\n    Every time data passes through a heavy mathematical layer like Self-Attention, the\noriginal meaning of the text is warped, filtered, and altered.\n\n- The Attention Block is designed to extract context (e.g., how words relate to each\nother).\n- However, in doing so, it can easily corrupt or wash away the core identity of the original\nword.\n\n>> CORRECTION: what the skip path actually carries <<\n    The residual carries x, the RAW pre-norm residual stream - NOT the RMSNorm output.\nThe formula is:   x = x + Attn(RMSNorm(x))\nRMSNorm produces a temporary normalized copy that feeds ONLY the attention block.\nThe untouched x bypasses everything and is what gets added back. The residual stream\nis deliberately never normalized in place - it is the model's persistent memory highway.\n    Mathematically you are saying:\n    \"Give me the deep contextual clues we just discovered, but mix them with a pure copy\nof what the words originally were so we don't forget our starting point.\"\n\n2. It Creates a \"Mathematical Highway\" for Gradients\n    During training (the step where the model learns), error signals called gradients must\ntravel backward through dozens of layers to update the weights.\n\n- The Problem: Passing a gradient backward through the chaotic math of Multi-Head\nAttention, Softmax, and W_o projections acts like a blender. By the time the signal\nreaches the early layers, it gets crushed down to zero (the Vanishing Gradient Problem).\n\n- The Solution: Because adding two matrices together has a calculus derivative of\nexactly 1, the plus sign (+) acts as a frictionless mathematical highway. The learning\nsignals can bypass the messy attention blocks entirely, flowing straight down the skip\nconnections all the way to the front of the model. This allows incredibly deep models (like\n70B or 405B parameter models) to train without their signals degrading.\n(Note: this benefit is TRAINING-only. At inference the residual is purely about\npreserving token identity across 32 layers.)\n\n3. What the Addition Looks Like Numerically\n    Because both matrices have the exact same shape ([1000 x 4096]), the GPU\nperforms an incredibly fast element-wise addition.\n\n    Imagine looking at a single value inside a token vector:\n\n- Original residual-stream value, PRE-norm: 1.5000 (the token's running identity)\n- Out-Projection Attention Value: -0.2300 (The subtle contextual tweak discovered by\nthe 32 heads)\n- Combined Final Value: 1.5000 + (-0.2300) = 1.2700\n\nThe original token's core meaning is preserved, but it has been subtly steered and\nenriched by the attention mechanism.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
