      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. The Final Gate: Post-Transformer RMSNorm\n    Before the final vectors are decoded, the GPU runs them through one last, standalone RMSNorm layer sitting at the top of the stack. This scales\nand stabilizes the numerical values one final time after their long journey through the stacked blocks.\n\n2. The Un-Embedding: The LM Head Layer (Language Model Head)\n    The matrix—still shaped as [1000 x 4096]—hits a massive linear layer called the LM Head.\n\n- The Operation: The LM Head is the exact mathematical inverse of the Token Embedding layer you encountered at the very beginning.\n- The Matrix Multiplication: It multiplies your token vectors against a giant dictionary weight matrix that maps the 4,096 hidden dimensions back to the\nmodel's entire vocabulary space (e.g., 128,256 columns for Llama 3).\n            [1000 x 4096] x [4096 x 128,256] ----------> [1000x128,256]\n\n\n3. The Winner: Softmax & Argmax Selection\n    The output is a list of 128,256 raw scores (logits) for every token position.\n\n- The GPU runs a Softmax function across these scores to convert them into percentages. Each slot represents the mathematical probability of a\nspecific word being the correct next choice.\n\n- The system looks at the very last token position in your sequence and runs an Argmax function (or a sampling technique) to select the word with the\nhighest percentage.\n\nThat single winning Token ID is extracted, sent to the Detokenizer to turn from an integer back into a text string (like \"water\"), and displayed on your\nscreen.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. The Final Gate: Post-Transformer RMSNorm\n    Before the final vectors are decoded, the GPU runs them through one last, standalone RMSNorm layer sitting at the top of the stack. This scales and stabilizes the numerical values one final time after their long journey through the stacked blocks.\n\n2. The Un-Embedding: The LM Head Layer (Language Model Head)\n    The matrix—still shaped as [1000 x 4096]—hits a massive linear layer called the LM Head.\n\n- The Operation: The LM Head is the exact mathematical inverse of the Token Embedding layer you encountered at the very beginning.\n- The Matrix Multiplication: It multiplies your token vectors against a giant dictionary weight matrix that maps the 4,096 hidden dimensions back to the model's entire vocabulary space (e.g., 128,256 columns for Llama 3).\n            [1000 x 4096] x [4096 x 128,256] ----------> [1000x128,256]\n\n\n3. The Winner: Softmax & Argmax Selection\n    The output is a list of 128,256 raw scores (logits) for every token position.\n\n- The GPU runs a Softmax function across these scores to convert them into percentages. Each slot represents the mathematical probability of a specific word being the correct next choice.\n\n- The system looks at the very last token position in your sequence and runs an Argmax function (or a sampling technique) to select the word with the highest percentage.\n\nThat single winning Token ID is extracted, sent to the Detokenizer to turn from an integer back into a text string (like \"water\"), and displayed on your screen.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
