      "updated": 1785876468302,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "    At its core, the Softmax function is a mathematical formula that takes a \nlist of raw, random numbers (positive, negative, large, or small) and transforms \nthem into a clean list of probabilities (percentages) that add up to exactly \n1.0 (100%).\n    The formula itself looks like this:\n                Softmax(x_i)= \\frac{e^{x_{i}}}{\\sum _{j}e^{x_{j}}}\\)\n\nWhile that formula looks complex, the way the GPU executes it is incredibly \nsimple. It breaks down into two distinct phases: The Exponent Phase and \nThe Normalization Phase.\n\nLet's look at exactly how it works using the data from your attention pipeline.\n\nStep 1: The Exponent Phase (\\(e^{x_{i}}\\))\n    First, the GPU takes every number in your list and uses it as an exponent \nfor the mathematical constant \\(e\\) (Euler's number, which is roughly \\(2.71828\\)).\n\nUsing the values from Row 0 of your scaled attention matrix:\n    - Token 0 Score: -0.4880 --> 2.71828^-0.4880 = 0.6138}\n    - Token 1 Score: 0.7494 --> 2.71828^0.7494 = 2.1157\n    - Token 2 Score: 0.3925 --> 2.71828^0.3925 = 1.4807\n\nWhy does the GPU use exponents?\n    It eliminates negative numbers: Raising any number as an exponent of \\(e\\) \nguarantees the result is a positive number. You cannot have a negative probability \n(e.g., a -20% chance of paying attention doesn't make sense).\n    It acts as a contrast amplifier: It heavily exaggerates the differences between \nnumbers. Notice how a raw score of 0.7494 is roughly double 0.3925, but after \nthe exponent phase, 2.1157 is significantly larger than 1.4807. This helps the model \nmake sharp, decisive choices.\n\nStep 2: The Normalization Phase (\\(\\sum \\))\n    Next, the GPU adds up all the newly calculated positive exponent numbers to find \nthe grand total:\n                        Grand Total = 0.6138+2.1157+1.4807= 4.2102\n\nFinally, it divides each individual number by that grand total to get its true percentage:\n\n    - Token 0 Percentage: \\(0.6138 \\div 4.2102 = \\mathbf{0.1458 \\ (14.58\\%)}\\)\n    - Token 1 Percentage: \\(2.1157 \\div 4.2102 = \\mathbf{0.5025 \\ (50.25\\%)}\\)\n    - Token 2 Percentage: \\(1.4807 \\div 4.2102 = \\mathbf{0.3517 \\ (35.17\\%)}\\)\n\nIf you add those three outputs together (\\(14.58\\% + 50.25\\% + 35.17\\%\\)), they \nequal exactly 100%.\n\nSoftmax's Two Core Roles in an LLMAs your diagrams show, Softmax is so useful that \nthe model uses it in two completely different places for two completely different goals:\n\n1. Inside the Attention Engine (Context Mapping)\n    What it processes: The raw similarity scores between tokens (\\(QK^T / \\sqrt{d_k}\\)).\nThe Goal: It creates an Attention Map. It tells the model exactly how to divide up its \nfocus. For example, the output above tells the model: \"When processing Token 0, pull 14.5% \nof the information from Token 0, 50.2% from Token 1, and 35.1% from Token 2.\"\n\n2. Inside the LM Head (Word Selection)\n    What it processes: The raw vocabulary scores (logits) at the very end of the model.\nThe Goal: It creates a Next-Word Probability Distribution. It turns 128,256 chaotic scores \ninto clean percentages so the text generator can see that there is a 92% chance the next \nword is \"water\", a 4% chance it is \"river\", and a 0.001% chance it is \"hamburger\".",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "    At its core, the Softmax function is a mathematical formula that takes a \nlist of raw, random numbers (positive, negative, large, or small) and transforms \nthem into a clean list of probabilities (percentages) that add up to exactly \n1.0 (100%).\n    The formula itself looks like this:\n                Softmax(x_i)= \\frac{e^{x_{i}}}{\\sum _{j}e^{x_{j}}}\\)\n\nWhile that formula looks complex, the way the GPU executes it is incredibly \nsimple. It breaks down into two distinct phases: The Exponent Phase and \nThe Normalization Phase.\n\nLet's look at exactly how it works using the data from your attention pipeline.\n\nStep 1: The Exponent Phase (\\(e^{x_{i}}\\))\n    First, the GPU takes every number in your list and uses it as an exponent \nfor the mathematical constant \\(e\\) (Euler's number, which is roughly \\(2.71828\\)).\n\nUsing the values from Row 0 of your scaled attention matrix:\n    - Token 0 Score: -0.4880 --> 2.71828^-0.4880 = 0.6138}\n    - Token 1 Score: 0.7494 --> 2.71828^0.7494 = 2.1157\n    - Token 2 Score: 0.3925 --> 2.71828^0.3925 = 1.4807\n\nWhy does the GPU use exponents?\n    It eliminates negative numbers: Raising any number as an exponent of \\(e\\) \nguarantees the result is a positive number. You cannot have a negative probability \n(e.g., a -20% chance of paying attention doesn't make sense).\n    It acts as a contrast amplifier: It heavily exaggerates the differences between \nnumbers. Notice how a raw score of 0.7494 is roughly double 0.3925, but after \nthe exponent phase, 2.1157 is significantly larger than 1.4807. This helps the model \nmake sharp, decisive choices.\n\nStep 2: The Normalization Phase (\\(\\sum \\))\n    Next, the GPU adds up all the newly calculated positive exponent numbers to find \nthe grand total:\n                        Grand Total = 0.6138+2.1157+1.4807= 4.2102\n\nFinally, it divides each individual number by that grand total to get its true percentage:\n\n    - Token 0 Percentage: \\(0.6138 \\div 4.2102 = \\mathbf{0.1458 \\ (14.58\\%)}\\)\n    - Token 1 Percentage: \\(2.1157 \\div 4.2102 = \\mathbf{0.5025 \\ (50.25\\%)}\\)\n    - Token 2 Percentage: \\(1.4807 \\div 4.2102 = \\mathbf{0.3517 \\ (35.17\\%)}\\)\n\nIf you add those three outputs together (\\(14.58\\% + 50.25\\% + 35.17\\%\\)), they \nequal exactly 100%.\n\nSoftmax's Two Core Roles in an LLMAs your diagrams show, Softmax is so useful that \nthe model uses it in two completely different places for two completely different goals:\n\n1. Inside the Attention Engine (Context Mapping)\n    What it processes: The raw similarity scores between tokens (\\(QK^T / \\sqrt{d_k}\\)).\nThe Goal: It creates an Attention Map. It tells the model exactly how to divide up its \nfocus. For example, the output above tells the model: \"When processing Token 0, pull 14.5% \nof the information from Token 0, 50.2% from Token 1, and 35.1% from Token 2.\"\n\n2. Inside the LM Head (Word Selection)\n    What it processes: The raw vocabulary scores (logits) at the very end of the model.\nThe Goal: It creates a Next-Word Probability Distribution. It turns 128,256 chaotic scores \ninto clean percentages so the text generator can see that there is a 92% chance the next \nword is \"water\", a 4% chance it is \"river\", and a 0.001% chance it is \"hamburger\".",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.55,
      "index": "b97"
    },
