    {
      "x": 4450,
      "y": 6450,
      "width": 900,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "type": "text",
      "text": "ROTARY POSITION EMBEDDING (RoPE) - the stage this board was missing\n\nLlama 3 adds nothing positional to the token embeddings. Position is injected\ninside EVERY attention block, on Q and K only, after the QKV projection and\nafter the head split, immediately before Q.K^T.\n\n1. Where it runs\n   x -> RMSNorm -> QKV proj -> split heads -> RoPE(Q), RoPE(K) -> attention\n   V is never rotated. The residual stream is never rotated.\n\n2. What it does\n   Each head's 128 dims are treated as 64 (x, y) pairs. Pair j at position m\n   is rotated by angle m * theta_j, where\n        theta_j = base^(-2j/128),   base = 500,000 for Llama 3\n   (Llama 2 used base = 10,000. The larger base is what buys long context.)\n\n3. Why rotation instead of addition\n   After rotation the dot product q_m . k_n depends only on (m - n), so\n   attention sees RELATIVE distance. It also means a cached K stays valid no\n   matter how many tokens are appended later - this is precisely why the KV\n   cache is mathematically correct across decode steps.\n\n4. Prefill vs decode\n   Prefill: rotate all seq_len positions at once.\n   Decode:  rotate the single new token at position = cache_len.\n   Cached K is stored ALREADY ROTATED, so it is never re-rotated.\n\n5. Llama 3.1 long context\n   RoPE scaling (factor 8, low_freq_factor 1, high_freq_factor 4) stretches\n   the low-frequency bands out to 131,072 tokens. Llama 3.0 stops at 8,192.",
      "id": "dd0d033d-b159-4ecb-b51c-266f31dc1bb9",
      "index": "bDu",
      "seed": 2101990518,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 530017186,
      "updated": 1786348967984,
      "originalText": "ROTARY POSITION EMBEDDING (RoPE) - the stage this board was missing\n\nLlama 3 adds nothing positional to the token embeddings. Position is injected\ninside EVERY attention block, on Q and K only, after the QKV projection and\nafter the head split, immediately before Q.K^T.\n\n1. Where it runs\n   x -> RMSNorm -> QKV proj -> split heads -> RoPE(Q), RoPE(K) -> attention\n   V is never rotated. The residual stream is never rotated.\n\n2. What it does\n   Each head's 128 dims are treated as 64 (x, y) pairs. Pair j at position m\n   is rotated by angle m * theta_j, where\n        theta_j = base^(-2j/128),   base = 500,000 for Llama 3\n   (Llama 2 used base = 10,000. The larger base is what buys long context.)\n\n3. Why rotation instead of addition\n   After rotation the dot product q_m . k_n depends only on (m - n), so\n   attention sees RELATIVE distance. It also means a cached K stays valid no\n   matter how many tokens are appended later - this is precisely why the KV\n   cache is mathematically correct across decode steps.\n\n4. Prefill vs decode\n   Prefill: rotate all seq_len positions at once.\n   Decode:  rotate the single new token at position = cache_len.\n   Cached K is stored ALREADY ROTATED, so it is never re-rotated.\n\n5. Llama 3.1 long context\n   RoPE scaling (factor 8, low_freq_factor 1, high_freq_factor 4) stretches\n   the low-frequency bands out to 131,072 tokens. Llama 3.0 stops at 8,192."
    },
    {
      "x": 4450,
      "y": 7300,
      "width": 900,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "type": "text",
      "text": "GROUPED-QUERY ATTENTION (GQA) - Llama 3 is NOT plain multi-head\n\nLlama 3 8B has 32 query heads but only 8 key/value heads. Each group of 4\nquery heads shares one K head and one V head.\n\n1. Per-layer projection shapes (8B: hidden 4096, head_dim 128)\n   W_q : [4096 x 4096]  ->  Q : [seq_len, 32, 128]\n   W_k : [4096 x 1024]  ->  K : [seq_len,  8, 128]\n   W_v : [4096 x 1024]  ->  V : [seq_len,  8, 128]\n   W_o : [4096 x 4096]\n   K and V are one quarter the size of Q. Any diagram showing [.., 32, 128]\n   for K and V is describing Llama 2 7B / plain MHA, not Llama 3.\n\n2. KV cache impact (the entire point of GQA)\n   Cache per token per layer = 2 * 8 * 128 * 2 bytes (fp16) = 4 KB\n   Plain MHA would be 16 KB. Across 32 layers at 8k context:\n        GQA ~ 1.0 GB      MHA ~ 4.0 GB\n   Decode is memory-bandwidth bound, so 4x less cache traffic is roughly\n   4x faster token generation - the same reason the KV cache exists at all.\n\n3. How the kernel handles the mismatch\n   Naive path: repeat_kv() broadcasts each KV head 4x to line up with the 32\n   query heads. Real kernels (FlashAttention / PagedAttention) never\n   materialise that copy - 4 query heads simply index the same KV block.\n\n4. Bigger siblings: Llama 3 70B = 64 query heads, 405B = 128 query heads,\n   both still 8 KV heads.",
      "id": "38e4d5ae-c1d1-4c47-8d3f-449f60f33e61",
      "index": "bDv",
      "seed": 904707885,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 1486886782,
      "updated": 1786348967984,
      "originalText": "GROUPED-QUERY ATTENTION (GQA) - Llama 3 is NOT plain multi-head\n\nLlama 3 8B has 32 query heads but only 8 key/value heads. Each group of 4\nquery heads shares one K head and one V head.\n\n1. Per-layer projection shapes (8B: hidden 4096, head_dim 128)\n   W_q : [4096 x 4096]  ->  Q : [seq_len, 32, 128]\n   W_k : [4096 x 1024]  ->  K : [seq_len,  8, 128]\n   W_v : [4096 x 1024]  ->  V : [seq_len,  8, 128]\n   W_o : [4096 x 4096]\n   K and V are one quarter the size of Q. Any diagram showing [.., 32, 128]\n   for K and V is describing Llama 2 7B / plain MHA, not Llama 3.\n\n2. KV cache impact (the entire point of GQA)\n   Cache per token per layer = 2 * 8 * 128 * 2 bytes (fp16) = 4 KB\n   Plain MHA would be 16 KB. Across 32 layers at 8k context:\n        GQA ~ 1.0 GB      MHA ~ 4.0 GB\n   Decode is memory-bandwidth bound, so 4x less cache traffic is roughly\n   4x faster token generation - the same reason the KV cache exists at all.\n\n3. How the kernel handles the mismatch\n   Naive path: repeat_kv() broadcasts each KV head 4x to line up with the 32\n   query heads. Real kernels (FlashAttention / PagedAttention) never\n   materialise that copy - 4 query heads simply index the same KV block.\n\n4. Bigger siblings: Llama 3 70B = 64 query heads, 405B = 128 query heads,\n   both still 8 KV heads."
    },
    {
      "x": -4773,
      "y": 18120,
      "width": 1100,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "type": "text",
      "text": ">> CORRECTION: which stop token is which <<\nLlama 3 has two terminal IDs and they are not interchangeable:\n   128001 = <|end_of_text|>  (base / completion models)\n   128009 = <|eot_id|>       (end of an assistant turn - chat models)\n   128008 = <|eom_id|>       (Llama 3.1, end of message before a tool call)\nThe panel above prints \"<|eot_id|> (Slot 128001) = 98.5%\", which pairs the\nchat token's name with the base token's ID. An instruct server almost always\nstops on 128009, and must test the whole SET, not a single == compare:\n   stop_token_ids = {128001, 128009}   (+ 128008 for 3.1 tool use)\n   if token_id in stop_token_ids: break\nAlso: the loop stops on the SAMPLED id. With temperature > 0 a 98.5% EOS is\nnot a guarantee - sampling can still emit another word.",
      "id": "4e2f84bc-3a44-4b7f-9463-c556f4fa13ee",
      "index": "bDw",
      "seed": 1108991326,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 313370466,
      "updated": 1786348967984,
      "originalText": ">> CORRECTION: which stop token is which <<\nLlama 3 has two terminal IDs and they are not interchangeable:\n   128001 = <|end_of_text|>  (base / completion models)\n   128009 = <|eot_id|>       (end of an assistant turn - chat models)\n   128008 = <|eom_id|>       (Llama 3.1, end of message before a tool call)\nThe panel above prints \"<|eot_id|> (Slot 128001) = 98.5%\", which pairs the\nchat token's name with the base token's ID. An instruct server almost always\nstops on 128009, and must test the whole SET, not a single == compare:\n   stop_token_ids = {128001, 128009}   (+ 128008 for 3.1 tool use)\n   if token_id in stop_token_ids: break\nAlso: the loop stops on the SAMPLED id. With temperature > 0 a 98.5% EOS is\nnot a guarantee - sampling can still emit another word."
    },
    {
      "x": -2251,
      "y": 16040,
      "width": 1000,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "type": "text",
      "text": ">> CORRECTION: two details about the LM Head <<\n1. NOT tied weights. Llama 3's lm_head is its own [4096 x 128256] matrix,\n   trained separately from the embedding table. \"Inverse of the embedding\n   layer\" is a conceptual analogy only (GPT-2 and Gemma tie; Llama does not).\n2. The slicing happens BEFORE this matmul, not after. Engines take the last\n   hidden row first:  [1 x 4096] x [4096 x 128256] -> [1 x 128256].\n   Computing all 1000 rows and discarding 999 costs ~1000x the work, and is\n   only done when logprobs for the whole prompt are requested.",
      "id": "c18c628c-cd48-4444-9f99-52fccccdfad3",
      "index": "bDx",
      "seed": 466470244,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 1978431422,
      "updated": 1786348967984,
      "originalText": ">> CORRECTION: two details about the LM Head <<\n1. NOT tied weights. Llama 3's lm_head is its own [4096 x 128256] matrix,\n   trained separately from the embedding table. \"Inverse of the embedding\n   layer\" is a conceptual analogy only (GPT-2 and Gemma tie; Llama does not).\n2. The slicing happens BEFORE this matmul, not after. Engines take the last\n   hidden row first:  [1 x 4096] x [4096 x 128256] -> [1 x 128256].\n   Computing all 1000 rows and discarding 999 costs ~1000x the work, and is\n   only done when logprobs for the whole prompt are requested."
    },
    {
      "x": -2251,
      "y": 18230,
      "width": 1150,
      "height": 225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "type": "text",
      "text": ">> CORRECTION: the real sampling order <<\nTemperature and top-k / top-p act on LOGITS, before Softmax - not after it:\n   logits -> / temperature -> top-k -> top-p -> Softmax -> multinomial draw\nSoftmax is re-normalised over the surviving candidates only, so the kept\nprobabilities still sum to 1.0.\nIf temperature == 0 the engine runs Argmax straight on the raw logits and\nskips Softmax entirely - Softmax is monotonic, so it cannot change the winner.\nRepetition / frequency penalties also apply at the logit stage.",
      "id": "2f8b0c84-a08c-495f-929c-10230c96eda2",
      "index": "bDy",
      "seed": 1015636860,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 117861154,
      "updated": 1786348967984,
      "originalText": ">> CORRECTION: the real sampling order <<\nTemperature and top-k / top-p act on LOGITS, before Softmax - not after it:\n   logits -> / temperature -> top-k -> top-p -> Softmax -> multinomial draw\nSoftmax is re-normalised over the surviving candidates only, so the kept\nprobabilities still sum to 1.0.\nIf temperature == 0 the engine runs Argmax straight on the raw logits and\nskips Softmax entirely - Softmax is monotonic, so it cannot change the winner.\nRepetition / frequency penalties also apply at the logit stage."
    },
